INT. BLUMENTHAL FARMHOUSE - SUNPORCH - LATE AFTERNOON

A glassed-in porch, ferns in coffee cans, a worn wicker couch. Dust motes spin in a long slant of gold. A crocheted blanket drapes the arm of the couch. The sound of crickets rises from the lawn.

EZRA BLUMENTHAL, 80s, sits in a rocker with a tartan shawl across his knees. His hands are spotted and still. His eyes are the pale blue of old china.

NELL BLUMENTHAL, 50s, his daughter, perches on the couch with a basket of mending, needle flashing. KIP, 9, her son, lies on the floor with a model airplane, flying it in circles above his head.

KIP
Grandpa, did you really build a plane?

EZRA
I built a glider.

NELL
Dad, don't.

EZRA
He asked.

KIP
How high did it go?

EZRA
Higher than the barn.

NELL
It crashed into the barn.

EZRA
(a soft laugh)
It crashed after the barn.

KIP
Did you get hurt?

EZRA
Only my pride.

NELL
And your leg.

EZRA
And the leg. Fine.

KIP
Can we build one?

NELL
Kip--

EZRA
Of course. We'll start Saturday.

NELL
Dad.

EZRA
Saturday.

The room goes quiet. Kip looks at his mother, then at his grandfather, then back at the plane in his hands. The crickets keep singing. Ezra gazes through the glass at the long grass, bending and straightening in the evening wind.

EZRA (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Saturday.

Nell stitches. She pulls the thread tight and bites it off. Her eyes are bright, and she does not look up.
